## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to combine a circular buffer with a monotonic deque to support rolling-window stats efficiently; checks edge-cases with duplicates and partial windows.

## Key Concepts

- Circular buffers
- Monotonic deque for max
- Running sum
- Amortized analysis

## Code Example

```javascript
class RollingWindow {
  constructor(n) {
    this.n = n;
    this.buf = new Array(n);
    this.head = 0; // points to oldest element
    this.size = 0;
    this.sum = 0;
    this.maxDQ = []; // decreasing values
  }
  push(v) {
    // evict oldest if full
    if (this.size === this.n) {
      const old = this.buf[this.head];
      this.sum -= old;
      if (this.maxDQ[0] === old) this.maxDQ.shift();
      this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.n;
      this.size--;
    }
    const idx = (this.head + this.size) % this.n;
    this.buf[idx] = v;
    this.size++;
    this.sum += v;
    while (this.maxDQ.length && this.maxDQ[this.maxDQ.length - 1] < v) this.maxDQ.pop();
    this.maxDQ.push(v);
  }
  sumWindow() {
    return this.sum;
  }
  maxWindow() {
    return this.maxDQ.length ? this.maxDQ[0] : undefined;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to a dynamic N resize while preserving amortized guarantees?
- How does the implementation behave with all equal values or negative numbers?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical string data structure knowledge for autocomplete systems. It's beginner-friendly but reveals depth in memory management and edge case handling.

## Key Concepts

- **Trie with dual counters**: Each node tracks pass and end counts to handle duplicate words correctly
- **Time complexity**: O(length) for all operations
- **Memory optimization**: Pruning unused nodes saves space
- **Pass count**: Number of words that pass through the node
- **End count**: Number of words that end at the node

## Code Example

```python
class TrieNode:
    def __init__(self):
        self.children = {}
        self.pass_count = 0
        self.end_count = 0

class Trie:
    def __init__(self):
        self.root = TrieNode()

    def insertWord(self, word):
        node = self.root
        for char in word:
            if char not in node.children:
                node.children[char] = TrieNode()
            node = node.children[char]
            node.pass_count += 1
        node.end_count += 1

    def deleteWord(self, word):
        node = self.root
        path = [node]
        for char in word:
            if char not in node.children:
                return
            node = node.children[char]
            path.append(node)
        if node.end_count > 0:
            node.end_count -= 1
        for node in path:
            node.pass_count -= 1

    def countWordsWithPrefix(self, prefix):
        node = self.root
        for char in prefix:
            if char not in node.children:
                return 0
            node = node.children[char]
        return node.pass_count
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a compact, eviction-aware data structure for time-windowed counting, a common pattern in real-time analytics.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window eviction via timestamped events
- Bucketed counts: map from count to set of users for fast top-k
- Invariants: update counts atomically during insertion and eviction
- Complexity: O(log n) updates, O(k log n) top-k retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowCounter {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.count = new Map(); // user -> cnt
    this.buckets = new Map(); // cnt -> Set<user>
    this.events = new Deque(); // (ts, user)
  }
  _addToBucket(cnt, user) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(cnt)) this.buckets.set(cnt, new Set());
    this.buckets.get(cnt).add(user);
  }
  _removeFromBucket(cnt, user) {
    if (!this.buckets.has(cnt)) return;
    this.buckets.get(cnt).delete(user);
    if (this.buckets.get(cnt).size === 0) this.buckets.delete(cnt);
  }
  prune(now) {
    while (this.events.length && this.events.peek().ts <= now - this.W) {
      const {ts, user} = this.events.shift();
      const c = this.count.get(user) || 0;
      if (c > 0) {
        this._removeFromBucket(c, user);
        const nc = c - 1;
        if (nc > 0) {
          this._addToBucket(nc, user);
          this.count.set(user, nc);
        } else {
          this.count.delete(user);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  addEvent(user, ts) {
    this.events.push({ts, user});
    this.prune(ts);
    const c = this.count.get(user) || 0;
    if (c > 0) this._removeFromBucket(c, user);
    const nc = c + 1;
    this._addToBucket(nc, user);
    this.count.set(user, nc);
  }
  topK(now, k) {
    const res = [];
    // iterate counts from high to low
    const counts = Array.from(this.buckets.keys()).sort((a,b)=>b-a);
    for (const cnt of counts) {
      const set = this.buckets.get(cnt);
      const users = Array.from(set).sort((a,b)=>a-b); // tie-breaker by userId
      for (const u of users) {
        res.push([u, cnt]);
        if (res.length === k) return res;
      }
    }
    return res;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support multiple simultaneous windows (e.g., W1 and W2) efficiently?
- How would you handle very large userId spaces with memory constraints, or concurrent updates in a multi-threaded environment?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests augmented BST design for duplicates and range queries on a dynamic multiset, focusing on balance, memory, and correctness.

## Key Concepts
- Treap with duplicates via per-node cnt
- Subtree size and sum augmentation
- kth smallest by counts; range queries via splits or traversal

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node{constructor(key,prio){this.key=key;this.prio=prio;this.cnt=1;this.l=null;this.r=null;this.sz=1;this.sum=key}};
function upd(n){if(!n)return; n.sz=n.cnt+(n.l?.sz||0)+(n.r?.sz||0); n.sum=n.key*n.cnt+(n.l?.sum||0)+(n.r?.sum||0)}
function rotateRight(y){const x=y.l; y.l=x.r; x.r=y; upd(y); upd(x); return x}
function rotateLeft(x){const y=x.r; x.r=y.l; y.l=x; upd(x); upd(y); return y}
function insert(n, key){if(!n)return new Node(key,Math.random()); if(key===n.key){n.cnt++; upd(n);return n}; if(key<n.key){n.l=insert(n.l,key); if(n.l.prio>n.prio) n=rotateRight(n);} else {n.r=insert(n.r,key); if(n.r.prio>n.prio) n=rotateLeft(n);} upd(n); return n}
function kth(n,k){if(!n) return null; const leftSize=n.l?.sz||0; if(k<=leftSize) return kth(n.l,k); if(k>leftSize+n.cnt) return kth(n.r,k-leftSize-n.cnt); return n.key;}
```

